<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<wml>

  <card id="imgupload" title="Raw PUT Image Upload">

    <!--
      WapForm for Web — <upload> tag demo page

      This page receives an upload request of a "completely different shape" from upload.wml; the two are not interchangeable:

        upload.wml   <upload>     multipart/form-data
                                  what a browser <form enctype="multipart/form-data">
                                  sends: several files at once, each with a field name
                                  and the original file name.

        imgupload.wml <upload> raw PUT
                                  the whole request body is the bytes of one image,
                                  with no wrapper, no field name and no file name.

      This tag was made to be compatible with the way upload.php receives files; the two behave the same:

        upload.php:
          $input = fopen('php://input', 'rb');   // read the whole body directly
          fwrite($output, $buffer);              // write it to a file as is

        imgupload.wml:
          <upload destination="..." nameconflict="unique" .../>

      The sender (UploadFile in Unit2.pas) already has this shape, so it needs no change:

        HttpCli.URL := 'http://localhost:8080/shop/imgupload.wml';
        HttpCli.ContentTypePost := 'image/jpeg'; // ← see "Always specify Content-Type" below
        HttpCli.SendStream := Stream;
        HttpCli.RequestVer := '1.1';
        HttpCli.Put;                             // ← the key is Put, not Post

      ★ Always specify Content-Type, and it must not be application/x-www-form-urlencoded.

      This is a pitfall found in testing, with subtle symptoms: the server "receives something" and the length is exactly right,
      but the bytes at the start of the file are altered and the saved image will not open. The reason: when Indy's HTTP
      server sees application/x-www-form-urlencoded it decides the body is form text,
      and processes it as a string once before handing it to anyone, applying Windows
      best-fit character conversion on the way: bytes are treated as Latin-1 characters, and when converted to the ANSI code page,
      anything without a mapping is replaced with the "closest-looking" ASCII letter —

        FF (ÿ) → 79 ('y')      D8 (Ø) → 4F ('O')      E0 (à) → 61 ('a')

      so the JPEG header FF D8 FF E0 becomes 79 4F 79 61, while bytes below $80
      (the JFIF letters) are untouched, so it looks as if "only the first few bytes are broken".
      This happens before the server reads the request, so neither the template nor <upload> can repair it;
      only the sender can fix it by specifying the right Content-Type.

      curl's data-binary option sends application/x-www-form-urlencoded by default,
      and so does ICS's THttpCli; both must set it explicitly. image/jpeg, image/png and
      application/octet-stream all work.

      Note that the other procedure in Unit2.pas, UploadFile2, uses a multipart POST and must call
      upload.wml, not this page — the two procedures map to two different tags, so don't mix them up.

      How the file name is chosen: a raw PUT request carries no file name at all (only multipart does),
      so the file name must be decided on the server. Three ways:

        1. filename="a02.jpg"
           A fixed file name. Like upload.php, uploading again overwrites the same file.

        2. nameconflict="unique"
           Generates a unique file name from a timestamp (yymmddhhnnsszzz), so repeated uploads do not
           overwrite each other. Same attribute name and meaning as <upload> in upload.wml.
           If filename is also given, only its extension is used and the name part is ignored.

        3. Neither
           Same as unique; the extension is inferred from the request's Content-Type
           (image/png is saved as .png, image/gif as .gif, anything else defaults to .jpg).

      Get the actual saved file name from the variable named by the savedname attribute (up_name on this page),
      because only the tag knows a generated file name, and the template needs it to show the preview.

      The file name can also come from outside, for example filename="$(name)" with ?name=... in the URL;
      the tag strips any path, blocks .. and drive letters, then checks the extension whitelist.

      Opening this page directly in a browser (GET) does not cause an error; since there is no body,
      result gets 'empty body', and the page shows the usage notes below.
    -->

    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="wf-head">
        <div class="wf-badge">WapForm for Web · Example</div>
        <div class="wf-title">Raw PUT Image Upload</div>
        <div class="wf-sub">The whole request body is one image, with no multipart wrapper — compatible with the way upload.php receives files.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="narrow">

      <upload destination="upload\"
                 nameconflict="unique"
                 accept="jpg,jpeg,png,gif,bmp"
                 result="up_err"
                 size="up_size"
                 savedname="up_name"/>

      <if cnd="up_size &gt; 0">
        <div class="res-bar">
          <span>✓</span>
          <span>Saved <b>$(up_name)</b>, $(up_size) bytes.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Save result</div>
          <img class="up-preview" src="upload/$(up_name)" alt="$(up_name)"/>
        </div>
      </if>

      <if cnd="up_err &lt;> ''">
        <div class="alert">
          <span>!</span>
          <span>Nothing was saved this time: $(up_err)</span>
        </div>
      </if>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">How to test</div>
        <div class="card-note">
          This is not a form page and has no file button — a raw PUT cannot be sent from an ordinary browser form;
          a program (or curl) must send the file content directly as the request body.
        </div>

        <div class="alert">
          <span>!</span>
          <span>
            <b>Always specify Content-Type</b> when sending (for example <code>image/jpeg</code>);
            do not use the default <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> —
            it makes the server treat the image as form text: the file length looks right, but a few
            bytes are silently altered and the saved image will not open. curl's <code>--data-binary</code>
            and ICS's <code>THttpCli</code> both send that type by default, so both must set it explicitly.
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="card-note">
          <b>With Unit2.pas:</b> change the URL in UploadFile to this page and press the button.
        </div>
        <div class="code-block">
          HttpCli.URL := 'http://localhost:8080/shop/imgupload.wml';<br/>
          HttpCli.ContentTypePost := 'image/jpeg';<br/>
          HttpCli.SendStream := Stream;<br/>
          HttpCli.RequestVer := '1.1';<br/>
          HttpCli.Put;
        </div>

        <div class="card-note">
          <b>With curl:</b>
        </div>
        <div class="code-block">
          curl -X PUT --data-binary "@C:\Wapform\a02.jpg" -H "Content-Type: image/jpeg" http://localhost:8080/shop/imgupload.wml
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="foot">
        Layout comes from <code>wapform.htm</code>; the receiving logic comes from the <code>&lt;upload&gt;</code> tag (mywap.pas).<br/>
        It is a different upload shape from <a href="upload.wml">File Upload</a> — that page uses a browser form's
        multipart, this one a raw PUT, and the two tags are not interchangeable.<br/>
        More examples: <a href="chart.wml">Chart Components</a>, <a href="crosstab.wml">Sales Cross Analysis</a>,
        <a href="report.wml">Grouped Sales Report</a>, <a href="function.wml">Function Self-Test</a>.
      </div>
    </div>

  </card>

</wml>
